= The coincidence <sec-coincidence>

Let $c$ be the centre of $T_L$, $P$ the point where $T_R$ touches the top, and $Q$ the point
where $B_L$ touches the bottom. Then
$ |c P| = |c Q| = #fmt(data.length, d: 12) dots $
exactly: the two squared lengths are equal in the number field of the packing. The common
squared length has degree $6$, with minimal polynomial
#align(center, text(9pt)[#raw(data.minpoly_len2)])

`circle-coincidences.sage` found this coincidence by comparing all segments between special
points of the diagram: corners, centres and points of tangency. It discards equalities that
hold for local or symmetric reasons, and this one survived. The search then asked which
tangencies the coincidence needs. It drops each of the $19$ contacts in turn, moves along the
resulting one-parameter family, and tests the coincidence there. It survives dropping any of
the four contacts of $I$, and fails when any other contact is dropped. So it does not
involve $I$ at all.

= The family of five circles <sec-family>

Remove $I$. The five remaining circles have $15$ contacts and $16$ unknowns (a centre and a
radius each, and $W$), so they move in a one-parameter family. The gap where $I$ was is now
a quadrilateral, bounded by $T_L$, $T_R$, $B_R$ and $B_M$. Take $r(T_L)$ as the parameter.
For $r(T_L)$ between $0.253$ and $0.413$, `circle-porism.sage` solves for the other circles
and compares $|c P|$ with $|c Q|$. They agree to the last digit, $10^(-16)$, at every member.
For $0.313 <= r(T_L) <= 0.353$ the configurations are honest packings; outside that range
the circles overlap, but the equality persists.

